**assistant**:
> To fix the GPG error you're encountering during `apt-get update`, you need to add the public key that is missing. Follow these steps:
> 
> 1. **Add the Missing GPG Key**:
>    Open a terminal and run the following command, replacing the `<KEY_ID>` with the missing key ID from the error message (`D155B8E6A419C5BE`):
> 
>    ```bash
>    sudo apt-key adv --keyserver keyserver.ubuntu.com --recv-keys D155B8E6A419C5BE
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Update the Package Lists**:
>    After adding the key, run the following command to update your package lists:
> 
>    ```bash
>    sudo apt-get update
>    ```
> 
> 3. **Check for Errors**: 
>    If you don’t see any error messages, the issue is resolved. You can proceed with your package installations or upgrades.
> 
> ### Alternative Method (Using Curl)
> 
> If the above doesn't work, you can also manually download and add the key:
> 
> 1. **Install `curl` (if not already installed)**:
>    ```bash
>    sudo apt-get install curl
>    ```
> 
> 2. **Download and Add the Key**:
>    ```bash
>    curl -fsSL https://your-key-url/D155B8E6A419C5BE.gpg | sudo gpg --dearmor -o /etc/apt/trusted.gpg.d/qgis.gpg
>    ```
> 
> 3. **Repeat the Update**:
>    ```bash
>    sudo apt-get update
>    ```
> 
> If you continue to experience issues, please let me know!
